{
  "text": "2011A12 Compare\nand contrast a single twitch and a tetanic contraction in a skeletal muscle\nfibre.\nList:\n·\nSkeletal muscle\n·\nGraphs: potential difference and force of\ncontraction\n·\nSingle twitch vs tetanic contraction (note most\nmarks here)\nSkeletal muscle:\nSarcomere\nExcitation and contraction\n·\nAction potential at motor nerve terminal\n·\nDepolarisation of post-junctional membrane\n·\nActivation of L-Ca\n2+\nchannels\n·\nCa\n2+\ninflux\n·\nCa\n2+\ntriggered Ca\n2+\nrelease from\n  sarcoplasmic reticular L-Ca\n2+\nchannel\n·\nCa\n2+\nbinds troponin C\n·\nTroponin removes tropomyosin from actin\n·\nActin-myosin cross-bridge cycling occur\nGraphs:\nPotential difference vs time\nNote short refractory period (~3msec) cf. cardiac\n  muscle (~250msec)\nForce vs time\nSingle twitch vs\ntetanic contraction:\nSingle twitch\nTetanic contraction\nDefinition\n·\nSingle contraction then relaxation\nContinuous fused contraction\nCause of onset\n·\nSingle nerve action potential\n·\nRepeated nerve action potential and cross-bridge cycling\n·\nTime between APs shorter than time to peak force\n·\n-Slow twitch 100ms duration hence >>10Hz\n·\n-Fast twitch 10ms duration hence >>100Hz\nCause of offset\n·\nACh metabolism and reuptake\n·\nCa\n2+\nreuptake and extrusion\n·\nCessation of nerve action potential\n·\nThen ACh metabolism and uptake\n·\nCa\n2+\nreuptake and extrusion\nDuration\n·\nSame each time\n·\n10-100msec\n·\n(Fast -> slow fibre)\n·\nVariable.\n·\nUntil fatigue or voluntary cessation\nForce developed\n·\nSame each time\n·\nLess\n·\n4x ↑tension possible\nInertia to be overcome\n·\nMore\n·\nLess (only present at the start)\nEnergy consumed overall\n·\nLess overall\n·\nMore per time\n·\nMore overall\n·\nLess per time\nEffect of depolarizing NMBD\n·\n↓Height\n·\nNo fade\nEffect of non-depolaring NMBD\n·\n↓Height\n·\nFade\nSensitivity for detecting residual blockade\n·\nLow\n·\nVery high\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
